          "thm-dominated-convergence": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Fix the scalar shell parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ and all shell, Fourier, and metric conventions of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and $\\Box=-\\partial_t^2+\\Delta_{\\mathbf x}$. For $\\phi\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define the partial spatial Fourier transform\n$$\\widehat\\phi_t(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\phi(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x$$\nand define $H_\\mu\\in\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^4)$ by\n$$\\langle H_\\mu,\\phi\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R}dt\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)}e^{-i\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)|t|}\\widehat\\phi_t(-\\mathbf k).$$\nThis is a continuous distributional extension of the free scalar time-ordered two-point function: on $t>0$ its kernel is the Wightman two-point distribution $W_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)$, and on $t<0$ it is $W_\\mu(-t,-\\mathbf x)$. The displayed partial Fourier formula fixes the extension at $t=0$; no product of a step function with an unspecified singular distribution is used. The kernel is symmetric under $(t,\\mathbf x)\\mapsto(-t,-\\mathbf x)$, agrees with the two orderings on spacelike-separated supports, and obeys\n$$ (\\Box-\\mu^2)H_\\mu=i\\delta_0 $$\nin $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^4)$. There is no extra prefactor $i$ in the definition of $H_\\mu$.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted complete lecture notes, Chapter 2, §§2.2 and 2.7.1",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S2.html",
              "locator": "Complete HTML text of Chapter 2; free scalar mode expansion, the time-ordered two-point function, and the Feynman propagator, equations 2.93–2.127 and 2.165–2.170."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted 2006 prepublication book draft, §§3 and 8",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page draft; §3, equations 3.7–3.16, and §8, equations 8.11–8.15. Srednicki's propagator includes a prefactor i; the carrier here does not."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-free-action-equations",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Classical free action variations and normalization",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#tempered-time-ordered-scalar-two-point-carrier",
        "design_state": "Planned compact-variation scalar and commuting-spinor action equations; both directions authored.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-dirac-projections",
          "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
          "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls",
          "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-dirac-projections": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Fix $\\mu\\ge0$, $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\\Box=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b$, and the Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]]; $\\mu$ is the inverse-length mathematical shell parameter of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. For a smooth real scalar field $\\phi$ and smooth commuting complex spinor $\\psi:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$, use the normalized free Lagrangian densities\n$$\\mathcal L_\\phi=-\\frac12\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi\\,\\partial_b\\phi+\\mu^2\\phi^2\\right),\\qquad \\mathcal L_D=\\bar\\psi\\left(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu\\right)\\psi,\\qquad \\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta.$$\nFor compactly supported smooth real variations $h$ and compactly supported smooth complex spinor variations $\\chi$, the action symbols are used only through the following finite, compactly supported first variations; no global integrability of the background fields is assumed:\n$$\\delta S_\\phi(\\phi;h)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}h(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi\\,d^4x,\\qquad \\delta S_D(\\psi;\\chi)=2\\operatorname{Re}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\chi^\\dagger\\beta\\left(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu\\right)\\psi\\,d^4x.$$\nEach field is stationary under all such variations if and only if its corresponding Euler–Lagrange equation holds pointwise:\n$$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi=0,\\qquad (i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0.$$\nThe adjoint Dirac equation follows from the second equation because the fixed matrices satisfy $(\\gamma^a)^\\dagger\\beta=\\beta\\gamma^a$. These are mathematical variation identities for commuting classical fields; they assert no quantization, Grassmann calculus, or physical measurement.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted complete lecture notes, §1.1.1",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S1.html",
              "locator": "Complete HTML Chapter 1, §1.1.1, equations 1.6–1.15."
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted complete lecture notes, §§4.2–4.3",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S4.html",
              "locator": "Complete HTML Chapter 4, Dirac adjoint and action (4.342–4.357), then independent spinor variations and Dirac equations (4.358–4.359). Tong uses the opposite metric signature to the fixed local convention."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted 2006 prepublication book draft, §§3 and 36",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page draft; §3, equations 3.7–3.10, and §36, equations 36.1–36.11. The spinor discussion uses anticommuting fields; the argument here concerns commuting classical fields."
            }
          ]
        }
